#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153124} :activity/id #uuid "ffe0a9ff-b89d-5315-a9ba-afafd34b3fa7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049229 :topic/id #uuid "e52ab899-5fcb-50d2-afee-4830a70f16df" :topic/math-academy-id 2616} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153391 :tutorial/content "We know how to define the double integral of a function over a rectangular domain. Now, we'll learn how to define a double integral over a non-rectangular region.\n\nLet's consider the non-rectangular region $D$ enclosed by the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ in the first quadrant, as shown below. \n\n![](images/e8/e80bcbaff16282f00e7e3df487d78cfbc51bb2e9660dde5e2fc421cd4d70a9af.png)\n\n \n\nWe will define the following double integral over this non-rectangular region:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} (3-x^2-2y^2) \\, \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nTo do this, we will use the following trick. Let's enclose the region $D$ by a rectangle $R$ with sides parallel to the coordinates axes, as shown below. \n\n![](images/21/214db95d949a8658e3fdbfea8f54c0e3bf914c11d9ef88e1ca4264d64d360c90.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we extend our function to $R$ by setting a new function $f(x,y)$ that's equal to $0$ outside $D$ and equals $3-x^2-2y^2$ on $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ab/ab8eed93d71415d810f4c8c9b19d690317769e270805a6925d35f5f0e4c9671f.svg)\n\n![](images/43/43288ccc496832a1a78f9fcaaabb4a5841fc79db5d2082dff7d4177005b61d01.png)\n\n \n\nAlthough $f(x,y)$ might not be continuous at the boundary of $D,$ the double integral of $f(x,y)$ over the rectangular domain $R$ will exist. \n\nTherefore, we define our double integral of $3-x^2-2y^2$ over $D$ as \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (3-x^2-2y^2) \\, \\textrm{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, the value of the double integral will give us the volume of the solid bounded by the surface $z=3-x^2-2y^2$ over the quarter-circular domain $D.$\n\n**Watch out!** The domain $R$ must not necessarily be the smallest possible rectangle that covers the region $D.$ It can be any rectangular domain that covers $D$ completely." :tutorial/id #uuid "14a20ee8-f248-507f-bce6-8dc6be91b9e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4246 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e5df5591-1e92-560d-ba7c-bbf286cf8b6b" :step/math-academy-id 25787 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152198 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152005 :question/id #uuid "5fe8dafe-b03c-54b6-a737-6572f59b97f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6995"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ad76e6b-acef-5221-8852-71dc42625bf3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482597}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Correct Function Used to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"} :step/id #uuid "9b86ebc6-eb40-565f-a521-f246f439bdd2" :step/math-academy-id 25788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152199 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152006 :question/id #uuid "80f011ae-c472-5dfa-8303-70396ecf51fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759218615282